WEBVTT

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:24.170
At the very bottom of the world, there's a cheerless land where the long night is 125 degrees below zero and murderous winds howl at 200 miles an hour across a desert as desolate as the moon.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:29.970
This is Antarctica, the seventh continent, five and a half million.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:37.280
million square miles of nothing but rock, ice, and drifting snow. No animal lives on

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:45.600
the land, no tree, nothing green, only men in the awful cold. The only source of heat,

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.500
the fuel they could bring with them.

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:21.000
A man can see the roundness of the earth.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:23.800
His eyes can trace its lakes and oceans

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:26.680
and the shape of the green lands of its continents.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:29.970
From that far out point in...

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.420
contrast to the greens and blues of land and water. He can note the stark whiteness of the ice-capped

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:43.470
polar regions. To a spaceman, the top and bottom of the earth look much alike. But a closer look

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:50.410
shows the Arctic, the North Polar region. To be an ocean surrounded by land, melt the ice at the

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:57.960
North Pole and you have melted ice, water. On the other hand, Antarctica, the South Polar region,

00:01:58.200 --> 00:01:59.720
is land, a continent.

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.980
a continent twice as big as the United States.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:05.900
Remove the ice from the bottom of the world,

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:09.210
and there remains a large continental land area.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.300
Above 60 degrees north latitude,

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.420
about a million people and many land animals live.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:19.850
There are forests and lumbering, mining and fishing industries.

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:23.280
But below 60 degrees south latitude,

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:24.670
until a few years ago,

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:27.020
there was not a single human being.

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:29.980
Nothing grew, and there was no...

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:39.660
land creature bigger than a bug. The Antarctic, exposed to fierce winds, is surrounded by ice

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:48.180
most of the year. Summer comes along in October and the sun shines, or tries to, 24 hours a day

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:56.550
for the next five months or so. It's the coldest place on earth. So cold that 95% of all the ice

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:58.020
in the world is here.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.480
weak sun has little effect on the ice, which is piled up for centuries. If we melted this

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:11.320
ice, all the seven seas would rise 200 feet. The seaports of the world would be drowned.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:17.750
The Antarctic continent itself, with the awful weight of ice removed, would rise 700 feet.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:26.050
The humidity is as low as the temperature. Rain is almost unheard of. And the raging blizzards

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:29.980
are much like desert sandstorms, blowing old snow with tremendous...

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:38.410
force. Of course, on occasion, it actually snows. But nevertheless, the Antarctic is really as dry

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:46.770
as the Sahara Desert. Why is it that man fights vicious elements to live here? Why? Because

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:52.950
Antarctica is a scientific laboratory, and ever curious to know, man is here on the forbidding

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:58.050
seventh continent to ask questions. But to learn, he must first survive.

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:12.150
Until recently, the chief way to survive was by shipping millions of gallons of petroleum fuel, thousands of miles.

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:17.470
Oil provided heat. Oil generated electricity for light.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:21.980
Oil furnished power for communications and scientific equipment.

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:28.290
Until other scientists and engineers produced a more convenient source of heat and power.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:29.960
On that day...

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:34.330
The Atomic Age and the Ice Age met.

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:47.390
Antarctica has been here, buried under ice.

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:48.230
We do not know.

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:50.690
It was not discovered until 1820.

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:53.920
No one spent a winter here until 1899.

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:59.730
As late as 1951, almost two-thirds of the continent was still unexplored.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:07.260
crackless wasteland was crossed and recrossed by questing men, and thus man's knowledge of an

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:25.290
unknown part of his world was greatly increased. Science and man's desire to know made great strides

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:29.200
during the International Geophysical Year, the IGY of 1957.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:37.100
58, following the I.G.Y., an international treaty was drawn up by member nations of the I.G.Y.,

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:42.230
a treaty formally recognizing that it is in the interest of all mankind

00:05:42.230 --> 00:05:47.980
that Antarctica shall continue forever to be used exclusively for peaceful purposes.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:53.170
And so, representatives of Russia, the United States, and ten other nations

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:59.310
fixed their signatures to a treaty that's an inspiring example of what can be done by nations.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.660
when they truly pursue peace.

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:07.340
Thus, the forbidding ice of this once mysterious land

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:10.830
became dotted with the outposts of scientific research.

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:16.300
Argentina, Belgium, Chile, Norway, and the United Kingdom

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:18.420
have stations on the Palmer Peninsula.

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:26.570
Along the coast, we find France, Japan, Australia, New Zealand, South Africa.

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:29.960
Russia has four bases, three inland and one inland.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:36.660
on the coast the united states maintains five stations all year round pole station is at the

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:43.820
geographic south pole new bird station in the heart of marie bird land the third is hallet

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:50.390
a station jointly operated by scientists of new zealand and the united states eights station is

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:57.020
located in the ellsworth land and finally there is mcmurdo station largest and busiest of the five

00:06:58.230 --> 00:06:59.790
McMurdo, home

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.920
to more than 200 men in the sunless Antarctic winter

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:05.410
is the center of scientific investigation

00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:08.410
and the main support base for all the American stations.

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:11.690
Planes of all sizes and many nations

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:14.370
operate in and out of McMurdo's Williams Field,

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:16.530
some 3 1⁄2 miles offshore,

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:20.540
on ice that once a year or so breaks up and goes to sea.

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:29.980
Russian scientists have landed.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.090
for an overnight stay at McMurdo, en route to their station, Father Inland.

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:41.100
Some 200 American scientists from more than 25 universities, foundations, and government agencies

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:45.010
are putting Antarctica through a microscopic examination.

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:53.540
They are training on it the mental lenses of biology, photography, geology, glaciology,

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:59.970
gravitology, meteorology, seismology, and upper atmosphere physics.

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:05.800
At McMurdo, the Ross Ice Shelf, about the size of Spain,

00:08:06.470 --> 00:08:13.890
is the perfect laboratory for a glaciologist to study and record the growth, movement, and decay of polar ice.

00:08:15.860 --> 00:08:20.340
And the Antarctic is a tremendous outdoor laboratory for virgin biological research.

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:22.560
What does live here?

00:08:23.350 --> 00:08:28.430
How have the native organisms adapted themselves to survive the coldest cold on Earth?

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:35.670
What is the relationship between flora and fauna here, and plants and animals on neighboring continents?

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:41.000
Research in the Antarctica has become highly systematized.

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:44.160
Bacteria, for example, is studied by two universities.

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:48.100
Ohio State devotes its work to examining bacteria in the soil,

00:08:48.820 --> 00:08:51.820
while a second team of researchers from the University of California

00:08:51.820 --> 00:08:57.230
is studying bacteria from cold, freshwater ponds and warm, saltwater lakes.

00:08:59.000 --> 00:08:59.980
The gate-legged...

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:04.140
sea spider and his relatives are the subjects of special attention from the University of the

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:13.000
Pacific. And the Bernice Bishop Museum of Honolulu is conducting insect study. This friendly fellow

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:19.860
was dredged up from some 2,000 feet under the ice flows by scientists of Stanford University. Stanford

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:26.300
is now in its third year of studies in marine animals in the McMurdo area. For it is a paradox

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:29.920
that the Antarctic is the most lifeless land on earth.

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:33.480
But the Antarctic Ocean abounds with life.

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:44.170
And there she blows, the cry of the whaler, sounds in the cold water.

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:49.690
Home of the blue whale, most magnificent and awesome creature on the earth.

00:10:06.270 --> 00:10:13.550
The ship has found a fortune here, taking whales that have been known to reach more than 90 feet long and weighing 160 tons.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:36.580
whales in the Antarctic. Some were hunted, and others, like the killer whale, are hunters.

00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:43.130
The killer whale is a kind of a porpoise, far removed from his friendly cousins at Marineland.

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:50.720
As much as 30 feet long, the killer is said to be the world's most savage animal. Seals

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:55.240
are quite common, both the dangerous leopard and the gentle Weddell seal.

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:10.030
Wettel's seal lumbers in awkward fashion across the ice, apparently unafraid of humans.

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:15.340
He likes to poke his nose into things here and there, and frequently gets in everyone's way.

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:21.560
The scuas, often called the eagle of the Antarctic, is also a common sight.

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:27.310
It lives on fish topped off with an occasional penguin egg, or baby penguin.

00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:29.970
And then, of course, there's the...

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:43.280
real native of the land at the bottom of the world. Everybody's favorite. There are 17 different species

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:51.230
of penguin in the world. All are well dressed, but only two, the emperor and the Adelie, live in

00:11:51.230 --> 00:11:57.940
the deep Antarctic. The emperor grows almost four feet tall and has the dignity proper to his height.

00:12:06.680 --> 00:12:18.240
At Little Shorty, the pin-size Adeli is only a foot and a half high.

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:37.310
Well, he is a tremendous swimmer.

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:39.920
Naturalists call him an aquatic bird.

00:12:57.940 --> 00:12:59.160
Nine friends on land.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:01.200
know they belong to the sea.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:36.330
the sea level is rising or falling. Physicists are also studying the polar upper atmosphere,

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:44.510
particularly the aurora australis, air glow, cosmic radiation, sunspots, and other atmospheric

00:13:44.510 --> 00:13:49.740
disturbances. Their findings seem almost certain to be valuable in space exploration.

00:14:01.820 --> 00:14:05.740
Depths of the sea, as well as space, are probed by scientists,

00:14:06.020 --> 00:14:09.440
but the Antarctic is the only ocean that extends around the Earth,

00:14:09.910 --> 00:14:14.190
flowing into the three major oceans, Atlantic, Pacific, and Indian.

00:14:15.870 --> 00:14:18.570
The weather of the southern hemisphere originates in Antarctica.

00:14:19.540 --> 00:14:22.960
If we want to learn something about that weather, this is the place to begin.

00:14:24.490 --> 00:14:27.380
But at first glance, the job seems impossible.

00:14:28.160 --> 00:14:29.980
Long-range forecasts require...

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:35.610
a chain of weather stations which cover the continent. These must be manned, supplied and

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:40.630
kept up through the long winter night. Today we have only a handful of men and stations

00:14:40.630 --> 00:14:46.780
to do the job. But to these, we have added an automatic nuclear powered weather station.

00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:53.510
This station uses a radioisotope fueled generator, SNAP 7C, designed and built by the

00:14:53.510 --> 00:14:59.380
Martin Company's nuclear division as its power supply. It is untended, runs for years

00:14:59.380 --> 00:14:59.940
without up

00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:03.770
and broadcasts regular weather reports every six hours.

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:11.000
A similar nuclear-powered station has been broadcasting from the Arctic Circle since August of 1961.

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:17.850
Cartographers are engaged in making accurate maps essential for useful exploration of the continent.

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:25.670
Their electronic equipment on the ground refines earlier aerial maps, sometimes literally moving mountains.

00:15:33.380 --> 00:15:37.060
Seismologists are recording earth tremors obtained in Antarctica.

00:15:38.060 --> 00:15:40.640
Seismologic information transmitted daily to Washington

00:15:40.640 --> 00:15:45.110
is of value in the worldwide program of earthquake reporting and study.

00:15:46.840 --> 00:15:48.680
Geologists of the University of Minnesota

00:15:48.680 --> 00:15:51.520
are studying one of the largest mountain chains on Earth,

00:15:52.140 --> 00:15:55.930
measuring, examining, detecting rock differences.

00:15:57.600 --> 00:15:59.740
Geological information about the Antarctic.

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:06.320
has been collected for 50 years but it's still very broad in general a great deal more study is

00:16:06.320 --> 00:16:12.120
essential even though seven million cubic miles of ice pose a bit of a problem when it comes to

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:19.290
measuring the land beneath the daily achievements of science here are difficult to weigh and the

00:16:19.290 --> 00:16:25.420
work of a week or even a month is not likely to shake the world it may be years before there is

00:16:25.420 --> 00:16:29.880
much practical application of results, and only after hundreds and thousands of

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:59.740
observations and measurements are made checked and evaluated some 27 tons of american scientific records were shipped from antarctica during the international geophysical year the united states navy has the responsibility of planning and operating the complex support program that makes the american scientific activity possible given the code name operation deep freeze the navy directs this vital mission

00:17:00.280 --> 00:17:04.060
working with elements of the Air Force, the Army, and the Coast Guard.

00:17:04.940 --> 00:17:08.560
Of course, the Marine Corps and Navy Seabees are actively engaged, too.

00:17:12.330 --> 00:17:16.030
Involved each year are a dozen ships, thousands of men,

00:17:16.670 --> 00:17:17.470
three dozen aircraft,

00:17:17.850 --> 00:17:21.900
and transportation of tons upon tons of equipment and supplies.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:36.370
man in space, each scientist in Antarctica must be backed up by many, many other people.

00:17:37.800 --> 00:17:43.580
Without the supplies brought in by cargo vessels, without the tanker's load of fuel for heating,

00:17:44.350 --> 00:17:50.500
for electric generation, and for snowmelters and airplanes, without icebreakers to smash

00:17:50.500 --> 00:17:59.200
a watery path through six-foot-thick ice, without the Navy's C-130 Hercules, R-4D, and R-7V.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:02.700
without the Air Force's C-124 Globemaster

00:18:02.700 --> 00:18:05.000
to fly personnel and express cargo

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:08.920
the 2,200 long miles from New Zealand and into the interior.

00:18:09.400 --> 00:18:11.780
Without all of this Navy-directed support,

00:18:12.440 --> 00:18:14.800
Antarctica would return to what it was,

00:18:14.800 --> 00:18:20.260
an icy, lifeless, empty desert.

00:18:20.730 --> 00:18:23.880
And there would be no McMurdo.

00:18:24.400 --> 00:18:25.840
The town we have come to think of

00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:27.480
is the New York of the Deep South,

00:18:28.330 --> 00:18:29.970
the Deep Freeze South.

00:18:31.500 --> 00:18:33.920
Aside from the weather that never really is pleasant,

00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:36.560
McMurdo tries to be like any other town.

00:18:37.400 --> 00:18:39.440
We have our church with well-attended services.

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:44.370
The neighborhood banker is friendly and conservative as a banker should be.

00:18:45.630 --> 00:18:48.480
We have our hospital with dentists and doctors.

00:18:49.220 --> 00:18:56.400
Everything any other town has, except mothers, wives, and sweethearts.

00:18:57.520 --> 00:18:59.980
So we've learned, like the boys marooned in the south.

00:19:00.030 --> 00:19:09.560
Pacific, there's nothing like a dame. We have other problems, too. Heating our homes, for instance.

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:16.090
Heating oil must be shipped in through the ice at great expense, and the danger of fire is always

00:19:16.090 --> 00:19:24.370
with us. Water is another. It's very scarce. There is never enough for shaving. Out of necessity,

00:19:24.390 --> 00:19:29.850
the lost art of the manly beard is revived, and everywhere, beards flourish with tender care.

00:19:31.240 --> 00:19:34.000
The bathroom is another almost non-existent luxury.

00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:37.040
And even the shower is an infrequent luxury.

00:19:38.350 --> 00:19:42.670
Every drop of water must be gathered as snow and ice and melted.

00:19:43.780 --> 00:19:49.760
It's time-consuming, expensive, and of course, this uses more precious fuel.

00:19:51.260 --> 00:19:56.380
Fuel that's essential for the small generators producing a trickle of electricity for radio communications.

00:19:57.530 --> 00:19:59.350
Radar for aircraft control.

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:01.610
and radar for scientific purposes.

00:20:02.520 --> 00:20:06.740
So, in another word, Antarctica has a power shortage.

00:20:09.160 --> 00:20:13.140
This is why plans and preparation went ahead for atomic power at McMurdo

00:20:13.140 --> 00:20:17.490
to give light for the six-month night, heat for the long winter,

00:20:18.100 --> 00:20:20.000
and electricity for daily jobs.

00:20:21.180 --> 00:20:26.690
McMurdo Sound, faced by a unique problem, now has a unique answer.

00:20:27.980 --> 00:20:29.980
One summery December day in 19...

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:35.040
1861, the official Navy log of events carried big news in a routine entry.

00:20:35.880 --> 00:20:42.430
14 December, Arneb, escorted by Atka and Eastwind, begins unloading at McMurdo Sound.

00:20:42.770 --> 00:20:50.500
The biggest American city on the seventh continent was about to become its first nuclear-powered town.

00:20:57.240 --> 00:20:59.980
Here it was designed, built.

00:21:00.640 --> 00:21:08.630
tested and delivered in a little more than a year the navy's seabees swarmed to work only 15 months

00:21:08.630 --> 00:21:13.550
earlier under an atomic energy commission contract for the development of a nuclear power plant for

00:21:13.550 --> 00:21:29.360
remote locations the martin company's nuclear division had begun work the power plant was to be

00:21:29.360 --> 00:21:29.960
located on

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:36.250
the site of Observation Hill overlooking McMurdo, and its foundations were to include preparation

00:21:36.250 --> 00:21:42.540
for a second and identical nuclear plant to be installed beside it. There's no scientific

00:21:42.540 --> 00:21:47.810
evidence that frozen rock is extra hard to work, but the sweating sea bees would have

00:21:47.810 --> 00:21:57.550
been happier if it had been warmer as they got the foundations ready. BM3A is the official

00:21:57.550 --> 00:21:59.890
name of the plant. P for portable.

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:07.050
M for medium, a medium power output of 1,500,000 watts.

00:22:08.490 --> 00:22:14.160
The plant was prefabricated in the United States in sections to ease the construction in the Antarctic.

00:22:15.520 --> 00:22:20.380
This kind of nuclear power plant can be shipped or even airlifted to remote sites

00:22:20.380 --> 00:22:25.980
simply because it was designed and built in portable parts to be assembled on the site.

00:22:26.160 --> 00:22:29.140
The plant itself is housed in two buildings.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:35.740
Building on the left, in huge tanks, are the nuclear reactor and the equipment to generate steam.

00:22:36.570 --> 00:22:42.750
For safety, the reactor tanks are completely buried in a natural shield of crushed rock.

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:51.760
This extraordinary little metal cylinder is the core of the reactor.

00:22:52.360 --> 00:22:59.970
It does the job of millions of gallons of diesel oil, the fissioning of atoms of uranium in the core.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:06.340
produces heat, which turns water into steam. The steam is piped to the second

00:23:06.340 --> 00:23:13.110
building that houses the equipment to generate electricity. Safety, of course,

00:23:13.390 --> 00:23:18.230
has been uppermost in the design of the plant. Warning systems, monitoring

00:23:18.230 --> 00:23:24.310
devices, and control mechanisms underline safety. On top of all the safety

00:23:24.310 --> 00:23:28.540
features, the plant automatically monitors radiation and detects the

00:23:28.540 --> 00:23:29.980
approach of any unsightly

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:35.400
safe level. A warning system is sounded well ahead of actual danger, and plant

00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:41.090
personnel can pinpoint and correct the situation at once. As a final act, the

00:23:41.090 --> 00:23:48.250
plant can be instantly shut down if necessary. Hand-in-hand with safety goes

00:23:48.250 --> 00:23:52.570
dependability, a dependability built into the system with the latest

00:23:52.570 --> 00:23:57.440
transistorized circuits and component parts which are easily replaced as units.

00:24:01.276 --> 00:24:04.056
In the cold of the frozen world of the south of us.

00:24:04.476 --> 00:24:08.196
the pioneering nuclear power plant has taken on a big job.

00:24:10.108 --> 00:24:11.708
Power for Continent 7.

00:24:12.828 --> 00:24:13.928
Power for heat.

00:24:14.556 --> 00:24:16.056
and power for illumination.

00:24:17.916 --> 00:24:22.556
Power for the ham radio operator to put through radio phone calls to homes in the States.

00:24:22.908 --> 00:24:24.608
The only contact with loved one.

00:24:26.748 --> 00:24:27.848
Hour of Plenty.

00:24:28.316 --> 00:24:29.816
to melt snow and ice.

00:24:30.124 --> 00:24:31.364
into water applying.

00:24:32.332 --> 00:24:34.892
Power enough to prevent a shortage of fuel oil.

00:25:06.460 --> 00:25:10.200
140 years have passed since seal hunters in wooden ships

00:25:10.716 --> 00:25:12.716
first sighted the shores of the seventh continent.

00:25:13.020 --> 00:25:13.660
And soon,

00:25:14.204 --> 00:25:19.324
Because man, in his ingenuity, has learned to live and work in this inhospitable world.

00:25:19.964 --> 00:25:25.884
Great airliners will whistle their way across the South Pole safely above the screaming blizzards far below.

00:25:27.548 --> 00:25:29.948
Will there one day be homes and schools?

00:25:30.190 --> 00:25:32.150
and children in Antarctica?

00:25:33.700 --> 00:25:38.560
Will there be industry with its chambers of commerce,

00:25:39.080 --> 00:25:39.940
even tourists?

00:25:40.930 --> 00:25:42.190
It hardly seems possible.

00:25:46.670 --> 00:25:49.360
Yet, whatever form progress does take

00:25:49.390 --> 00:25:51.270
in this unfriendly land,

00:25:52.090 --> 00:25:58.100
nuclear power for heat, light, water, and electricity

00:25:58.100 --> 00:25:59.960
will be at hand.

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:01.240
to help.
